const props={"page": {"url": "/supporters/seaair/", "title": "SeaAir cabin cooling for the night after a long motor", "description": "On many cruising boats the engine box sits close to a sleeping space. Shut down after a long afternoon under power and the cockpit may cool well before that berth does. SeaAir supplies marine air conditioning and heating for suitable DC systems. The decision we're interested in is what happens after the diesel stops and the house bank takes over.", "schema": ["WebPage"], "pageType": "utility", "status": "ready", "indexable": true, "blockers": [], "headings": [], "faqs": [], "parent": "/supporters/", "parentTitle": "Featured sponsors"}};
const content="<article class=\"sn-page\" data-editorial-version=\"4\"><div class=\"sn-read\"><a href=\"/supporters/\">Featured sponsors</a><p class=\"sn-kicker\">Marine Diesel Engine \u00b7 Sponsor feature</p><img class=\"sn-logo\" src=\"/images/supporters/seaair/logo.png\" alt=\"SeaAir logo\" width=\"4791\" height=\"1529\" loading=\"lazy\"><h1>SeaAir cabin cooling for the night after a long motor</h1><p class=\"sn-intro\">On many cruising boats the engine box sits close to a sleeping space. Shut down after a long afternoon under power and the cockpit may cool well before that berth does. SeaAir supplies marine air conditioning and heating for suitable DC systems. The decision we&#x27;re interested in is what happens after the diesel stops and the house bank takes over.</p><img class=\"sn-hero\" src=\"/images/supporters/seaair/photo.webp\" alt=\"Marine air conditioning from SeaAir\" width=\"900\" height=\"900\" loading=\"lazy\"><section><h2>Ask about the berth at its warmest</h2><p>Give SeaAir the cabin dimensions and tell the team if it shares a bulkhead with the machinery space. Describe how the door is used overnight and where supply air could actually reach the mattress. Ask about the return-air path too. A thermostat reading in the saloon isn&#x27;t the same as a comfortable bunk at the far end of the boat.</p></section><img class=\"sn-hero\" src=\"/images/supporters/seaair/detail.webp\" alt=\"SeaAir touchscreen cabin control\" width=\"900\" height=\"900\" loading=\"lazy\"><section><h2>Control the overnight allowance</h2><p>SeaAir offers different operating modes, including Boost and Overnight, and lets you set a limit in amp-hours. We like that last feature because it makes the trade-off explicit. A skipper can allow for refrigeration, instruments and morning loads before deciding how much battery capacity to spend on cooling. Starting with a hot cabin will make a different demand from maintaining the temperature once the crew is asleep.</p><p>Talk through the charging arrangement with the installer as well as the air conditioner. Include bank voltage, usable capacity, typical motoring time and the next reliable charge. Bringing that information to SeaAir makes it easier to compare a system for your ordinary coastal weekend with one sized for a very different boat.</p></section><p><a rel=\"sponsored nofollow noopener noreferrer\" href=\"https://seaair.com/\">Discuss your cabin with SeaAir \u2197</a></p></div></article>";
